<subsection xml:id="subsection-1-5-1">
    <title>Optimizing a Function</title>
    <p>
        Consider the graph below:
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="extrema-graph">
        <caption>Local extrema of a function</caption>
        <image width="80%" xml:id="local-extrema-graph">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (-5,0)--(5,0);
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (.2,-5)--(.2,5);
                    \draw[domain=-1.5:0.8,smooth,variable=\x,blue,thick] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x*\x -0.75*\x*\x - 0.25*\x + 1});
                    \draw[thick,blue] (0.8,0.448)--(2,-3)--(3.7,2.47075);
                    \draw[blue] (-2.6,2) node{$f(x)$ on a closed interval};
                    \filldraw (-1.5,-1.15625) circle (2.5pt);
                    \filldraw (3.7,2.47075) circle(2.5pt);
                    \filldraw (-.155,1.02) circle(2.5pt);
                    \filldraw (0.8,0.448) circle(2.5pt);
                    \filldraw (2,-3) circle(2.5pt);
                    \draw(0.8,-.2)--(0.8,.2);
                    \draw(0.8,-.4) node{$c$};
                    \draw(-1.5,-.2)--(-1.5,.2);
                    \draw(-1.5,.4) node{$a$};
                    \draw(-.155,-.2)--(-.155,.2);
                    \draw(-.155,.4) node{$b$};
                    \draw(2,-.2)--(2,.2);
                    \draw(2,.4) node{$d$};
                    \draw(3.7,-.2)--(3.7,.2);
                    \draw(3.7,.4) node{$e$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        The graph represents a function <m>f(x)</m> on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>.  
        We would like to determine the numerical values of "local extrema" - the points that are larger or smaller than all other points around them.  
        Graphically, we see that there are local minimum at <m>x=a</m> and <m>x=d</m> and local maximums at <m>x=b</m> and <m>x=e</m>.  
        We will also be interested in the "global extrema" - the points that are larger or smaller than all other points on the function.  
        We see that a global maximum occurs at $x=e$ and a global minimum occurs at <m>x=d</m>.  
    </p>

    <p>
        The process of finding such extrema analytically (and by hand) requires the use of derivatives.  
        Consider the point at <m>x=b</m>.  
        If we drew a tangent line to <m>f(x)</m> at that point, the line would have slope <m>0</m>.  
        This means that <m>f'(b) = 0</m>.  
        Local minimums and maximums <em>can</em> occur at "bottoms of valleys" and "tops of hills,"" which are points at which <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.  
        Thus, finding where a function's derivative is equal to zero gives some candidates for local extrema.  
    </p>

    <p>
        Consider the point at <m>x=d</m>.  
        Here, the derivative does not exist.  
        These "sharp points" can also yield local extrema.  
        Hence, finding where a function's derivative does not exist gives more candidates for the local extrema.  
        Be warned that points at which the derivative is zero or does not exist do not always yield local extrema.  
    </p>

    <p>
        Take the point at <m>x=c</m> in the graph above.  
        The derivative does not exist at that point (slopes change suddenly), but it is not a local minimum or maximum.  
    </p>  

    <p>    
        Finally, if the function is on a closed interval, we can have local extrema at the endpoints (but not always).  
    </p>

    <p>
        Once we identify the points where local extrema <em>could</em> occur, 
        we need to determine if we in fact do have local minimums or maximums.  
        We could employ one of the tests (first-derivative or second-derivative) 
        or check numerically.  
        If one of these points is the largest or smallest values on the entire function, 
        then we have that those points are the global extrema.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-optimization">
        <title>Optimization Steps</title>
        <p>
            To find the local extrema of a continuous function <m>f(x)</m>:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Find the derivative function <m>f'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Find the values for which <m>f'(x)</m> is zero or does not exist.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Determine if these values represent local minimums or maximums (or neither).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        If on a closed interval, check if the endpoints are local extrema.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <example xml:id="ex-optimize">
        <title>Finding Local Extrema</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the local extrema and global extrema on the function <m>\displaystyle {f(x) = \frac{2}{3} x^3 - 2x^2 - 6x}</m> on the closed interval <m>[-4,4]</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                First, we find where <m>f'(x)</m> is zero or does not exist.  
                <m>{f'(x) = 2x^2 - 4x - 6}</m>. 
                This is a quadratic function, which always exists.  
                So, we just solve <m>{2x^2 - 4x - 6 = 0}</m>.  
                Using the quadratic formula gives <m>{x = -1}</m> and <m>x=3</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                So, we have four points that could be minimums or maximums: 
                <m>x=-4, -1, 3, 4</m> (we must include the endpoints).
            </p>

            <p>
                While not formal, any easy way to check if the points at 
                <m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=3</m> are extrema are to compare their function values with nearby <m>x</m> values.  
                For example, <m>f(-1.1) = 3.29</m>, <m>f(-0.9) = 3.29</m>, 
                and <m>f(-1) = 3.33</m>.  
                So, we have a local maximum at <m>(-1, 3.33)</m>.  
                In a similar fashion, we can find that we have a local minimum  at <m>(3,-18)</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                At <m>x=4</m>, we can see that smaller <m>x</m> values yield smaller function values.  
                For example <m>f(4)=-13.33>-14.27 =f(3.9)</m>.  
                So, we will have a local maximum at <m>(4,-13.33)</m>.  
                Similarly, we can get that we have a local minimum at <m>(-4,-50.67)</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Lastly, we just compare the four function values at are points.  
                We see that we have a global maximum at <m>(-1,3.33)</m> and a global minimum at <m>(-4,-50.67)</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-optimize-2">
        <title>Finding Global Extrema</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the maximum and minimum values of the function 
                <m>{\displaystyle g(x) = xe^{-2x}}</m> for <m>{x\geq 0}</m>, if any exist.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                First, we find <m>g'(x)</m>. 
                Using the product rule, we have <m>\displaystyle g'(x)</m> 
            </p> 

            <p> 
                <m>{\displaystyle = x\left(\frac{d}{dx}e^{-2x}\right) + e^{-2x}\left(\frac{d}{dx}x\right)} {= x\left(-2e^{-2x}\right) + e^{-2x}(1) = e^{-2x}(1-2x)}</m>. 
                First, note that this always exists. 
                So, we just solve <m>\displaystyle e^{-2x}(1-2x) = 0</m>. 
                Notice that <m>e^{-2x}\ne 0</m>. 
                So, solving <m>1-2x=0</m> for <m>x=0.5</m> yields our only solution.
            </p> 

            <p>
                We have <m>g(0.5) = 0.184</m> and values around <m>x=0.5</m> yield smaller function values (e.g. <m>g(0.4) = 0.180</m>). 
                So, we can conclude that we have a maximum at <m>(0.5,0.184)</m>.
            </p> 

            <p>
                We can also get that the endpoint <m>x=0</m> yields a minimum at <m>(0,0)</m>.
            </p> 
            
            <p>
                However, we need to determine the behavior of <m>g(x)</m> as <m>x</m> goes to infinity. 
                (It may rise above our proposed maximum at <m>x=0.5</m> or proposed minimum at <m>x=0</m>.) 
                For <m>x>0</m>, we see that <m>g(x) >0</m>. 
                Hence, our point at <m>(0,0)</m> is a global minimum. 
                Further, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}xe^{-2x}= 0</m>, which means the function tends towards <m>0</m>. 
                So, our point at <m>(0.5,0.184)</m> is a global minimum. 
                (We could have also noted that <m>g'(x)&#x3C;0</m> for <m>x>0.5</m>, which means <m>g(x)</m> decreases after our maximum at <m>x=0.5</m>.)
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</subsection>